---
/**
 * /products/essentials — Polaris Essentials, on the same layout /products/pro uses.
 *
 * The page is the record in data/products/essentials.ts plus the two blocks below,
 * which stay as markup on purpose:
 *
 *   spec-tables — the inline specification sheet. Every row carries its own
 *     citation and several carry [verify:] flags that initVerifyFlags and
 *     scripts/check-claims.py both look for. Turning it into data would mean
 *     retyping every ruled fact on the page, which CLAUDE.md forbids.
 *   upsell — the .g3band. Its argument is specific to this tier.
 */
import ProductShowcase from "../../layouts/ProductShowcase.astro";
import { ESSENTIALS } from "../../data/products/essentials";
---

<ProductShowcase {...ESSENTIALS}>
  <Fragment slot="spec-tables">
      <details class="mtbl" open><summary><b>Sharing</b><span class="mtbl-n">4 rows <i class="mtbl-c">&#9662;</i></span></summary><div class="famtbl mtbl-t"><table><tr><th style="width:44%">Specification</th><th>Polaris Essentials</th></tr><tr><td>Simultaneous shares in the workspace</td><td class="y">Multiple live sources composited side by side, laid out automatically as sources join and leave. The ceiling is 5, against 10 on Pro. It is the same compositor in both: the limit is memory on the smaller chassis, not a licence or a reduced build [Ruled by Damian Blazy 12 Aug 2026 - see src/data/rulings.ts]</td></tr><tr><td>Web-join from the browser: no app, no download</td><td class="y">&#10003;</td></tr><tr><td>Native AirPlay &middot; Miracast &middot; Google Cast (LAN)</td><td class="y">&#10003; all three, built in: cast from Apple, Windows and Chromebook devices on the room network with nothing installed [verify: BOTH released data sheets - Essentials DS-MCS.ESS-02192025 and Pro DS-MCS.PRO-05072025 - list protocols as WebRTC, AirPlay and Miracast only, with no Google Cast. Two published documents against one headline capability: engineering to confirm and the sheets to be reissued before launch]</td></tr><tr><td>Mobile sharing &middot; phones and tablets</td><td class="y">&#10003; the Polaris app for iOS and Android: share your screen, photos or a live whiteboard, and control the workspace from your seat</td></tr></table></div></details><details class="mtbl"><summary><b>Network</b><span class="mtbl-n">4 rows <i class="mtbl-c">&#9662;</i></span></summary><div class="famtbl mtbl-t"><table><tr><th style="width:44%">Specification</th><th>Polaris Essentials</th></tr><tr><td>802.1X network authentication</td><td class="y">&#10003; shipping. The data sheet lists wireless security as WPA2, WPA2 Enterprise or IEEE 802.1X &mdash; so an Essentials room authenticates to your NAC with a certificate exactly as a Pro room does. [Essentials data sheet DS-MCS.ESS-02192025] [verify: which EAP methods Essentials supports; Pro documents EAP-TLS, PEAP and TTLS and the data sheet does not break them out per tier - hardware team]</td></tr><tr><td>Cross-network sharing (no VPN)</td><td class="y">&#10003; included on every Polaris tier. Cloud-negotiated direct connections; signalling is outbound-only TLS on TCP 443, so there are no inbound firewall rules to request, and no VPN</td></tr><tr><td>Wireless security</td><td>WEP, WPA2, WPA2 Enterprise or IEEE 802.1X [WEP is listed because the released Essentials data sheet lists it and the radio will still negotiate it for legacy equipment. It is not recommended and Mersive does not advise deploying it: WEP has been cryptographically broken since 2001 and is trivially recoverable. Use WPA2-Enterprise or 802.1X with EAP-TLS. Ruled by Damian Blazy 12 Aug 2026: keep the mode listed, carry the caveat, highlight it]</td></tr><tr><td>Network interfaces</td><td>1&times; 10/100/1000 RJ-45 (Realtek RTL8211F gigabit PHY), no PoE; MediaTek MT7921: Wi-Fi 6 (802.11ax), 802.11 a/b/g/n/ac/ax, 2.4 + 5 GHz, 2&times;2 MIMO [Essentials data sheet DS-MCS.ESS-02192025]; Bluetooth (MediaTek MT7921) [verify: the data sheet states neither the bands nor the Bluetooth revision - hardware team]</td></tr></table></div></details><details class="mtbl"><summary><b>Security</b><span class="mtbl-n">7 rows <i class="mtbl-c">&#9662;</i></span></summary><div class="famtbl mtbl-t"><table><tr><th style="width:44%">Specification</th><th>Polaris Essentials</th></tr><tr><td>Encryption in transit</td><td class="y">Signalling and fleet management run outbound over HTTPS to Polaris Cloud. The device exposes no TLS listener of its own, so a port scan of the Mini finds nothing to negotiate against &mdash; the encryption that matters is on the outbound leg, not on an open port in your room [verify: the verification document proposes publishing that the cloud negotiates TLS 1.3 or 1.2 and refuses 1.0 and 1.1. That is a minimum-version claim, which this site&rsquo;s hard rule forbids until the 7 July 2026 finding is retested. Re-measure, then decide whether to state versions here - engineering and CISO]</td></tr><tr><td>Secure boot &middot; secure element</td><td class="y">&#10003; secure boot is mandatory on production firmware, not a setting; NXP SE050 secure element on board holds the device&rsquo;s own identity and update-verification keys in hardware &mdash; the same part as Pro. The secure-element build assigns an I&sup2;C bus per machine and fails the build outright for any machine it does not handle, so every shipping unit, Mini included, has one [verify: the firmware consistently names the SE05x family rather than the exact part; confirm SE050 specifically before this is used in a security questionnaire - hardware team and CISO]</td></tr><tr><td>TAA-compliant supply chain</td><td class="y">&#10003; built with Advantech in Taiwan, a TAA-designated country, with the bare printed circuit board the only China-sourced item in the build [verify: confirm the PCB is the sole China-sourced item, and that the fabricator is named on the country-of-origin attestation - supply chain] [final claim wording with legal]</td></tr><tr><td>Signed updates, no downgrade &mdash; and automatic rollback on a failed load</td><td class="y">&#10003; the same firmware mechanism as Pro, because it is the same distro: update packages are checked for authenticity and integrity before they are applied and a <b>downgrade</b> to an earlier version is refused, while two firmware partitions mean a load that fails to come up returns to the last known-good version [confirmed by Damian Blazy 14 Aug 2026]</td></tr><tr><td>Linux-hardened OS</td><td class="y">&#10003; purpose-built, minimised Linux at the display: no Windows attack surface, no general-purpose desktop</td></tr><tr><td>Physical: Kensington Nano Security Slot</td><td class="y">&#10003; on the chassis [Essentials data sheet DS-MCS.ESS-02192025]</td></tr><tr><td>Content protection</td><td class="y">&#10003; HDCP 2.0 [Essentials data sheet DS-MCS.ESS-02192025]</td></tr></table></div></details><details class="mtbl"><summary><b>Where Essentials stops: Pro territory</b><span class="mtbl-n">3 rows <i class="mtbl-c">&#9662;</i></span></summary><div class="famtbl mtbl-t"><table><tr><th style="width:44%">Specification</th><th>Polaris Essentials</th></tr><tr><td>True wireless BYOM (room AV bridged into your call)</td><td class="n">&mdash; Pro only</td></tr><tr><td>Display outputs</td><td class="n">One. Essentials drives a single display; Pro ingests and drives two &mdash; Pro only</td></tr><tr><td>HDMI input: wired ingest from a room system or a laptop</td><td class="n">&mdash; Pro only. The Mini has no HDMI receiver on the board, so this is a chassis difference rather than a licence</td></tr></table></div></details><details class="mtbl"><summary><b>I/O</b><span class="mtbl-n">5 rows <i class="mtbl-c">&#9662;</i></span></summary><div class="famtbl mtbl-t"><table><tr><th style="width:44%">Specification</th><th>Polaris Essentials</th></tr><tr><td>HDMI output</td><td class="y">1&times; HDMI 2.0 out &mdash; one display, where Pro drives two. 4K (4096 &times; 2160) at 30&nbsp;Hz; 1080p and 720p up to 30&nbsp;Hz [Essentials data sheet DS-MCS.ESS-02192025]</td></tr><tr><td>Ethernet</td><td class="y">1&times; 10/100/1000 RJ-45. No PoE on Essentials &mdash; the Pod takes PoE+, the Mini does not [Essentials data sheet DS-MCS.ESS-02192025]</td></tr><tr><td>Wi-Fi radio</td><td class="y">MediaTek MT7921: Wi-Fi 6 (802.11ax), 802.11 a/b/g/n/ac/ax compliant, 2.4 + 5 GHz, 2&times;2 MIMO [Essentials data sheet DS-MCS.ESS-02192025]; Bluetooth (MediaTek MT7921) [verify: the data sheet states neither the bands nor the Bluetooth revision. The MT7921 part integrates a Bluetooth 5.2 controller per MediaTek&rsquo;s own datasheet, but no Mersive source states a revision, so none is published here - hardware team]</td></tr><tr><td>Video codecs</td><td>H.264 encode and decode &middot; H.265 encode and decode &middot; VP8 decode &middot; AV1 decode &middot; JPEG/MJPEG decode &mdash; all required. VP9 decode is preferred rather than required. Hardware colour conversion: YUY2/YUYV [Mersive hardware requirement, rev. Nov 2024]</td></tr><tr><td>What the Mini does not have</td><td>No USB ports, no HDMI input and no audio-out jack: the rear panel is Kensington Nano Security Slot, Ethernet, HDMI 2.0 and DC power, and that is the whole of it. This is why wireless BYOM is a Pro capability rather than a licence away &mdash; there is no USB host to put a room camera on [Essentials data sheet DS-MCS.ESS-02192025]</td></tr></table></div></details><details class="mtbl"><summary><b>Compute</b><span class="mtbl-n">4 rows <i class="mtbl-c">&#9662;</i></span></summary><div class="famtbl mtbl-t"><table><tr><th style="width:44%">Specification</th><th>Polaris Essentials</th></tr><tr><td>Chipset / SoC</td><td>MediaTek MT8370 (Genio 510): ARM SoC with Mali GPU. The Gen&nbsp;4 Pod Mini ships as Polaris Essentials, and the shipping board identifies itself as MT8370 in its own kernel journal</td></tr><tr><td>NPU: on-device AI silicon</td><td>Requirement: minimum 2.0 TOPS, goal 3.0 TOPS [Mersive hardware requirement, rev. Nov 2024]. As built: 2 TOPS [confirmed by Damian Blazy 14 Aug 2026] &mdash; the SoC's APU. No inference runtime ships in the current image, so nothing on the device uses it yet [verify: the tflite DISTRO_FEATURE is commented out in mersive.inc:20-21 and is the only switch that pulls the runtime in. Confirm the roadmap for enabling it - engineering]</td></tr><tr><td>RAM &middot; storage</td><td>4 GB &middot; 32 GB eMMC &mdash; proven from the board device tree and the eMMC partition layout, and confirmed on a Mini in hand (board VEGA_DMS233)</td></tr><tr><td>Video pipeline: simultaneous decode</td><td>H.264 and H.265 encode and decode; VP8 and VP9 decode &mdash; the same codec surface as Pro, not a reduced one. Composites into a 4K (4096 &times; 2160) 30&nbsp;Hz output; 2+ concurrent 1080p30 decodes required, more supported; the ceiling is 5 simultaneous shares, set by memory on the smaller chassis rather than by licensing [Mersive hardware requirement, rev. Nov 2024: workload targets] [Ruled by Damian Blazy 12 Aug 2026 - see src/data/rulings.ts]</td></tr></table></div></details><details class="mtbl"><summary><b>Power &amp; physical</b><span class="mtbl-n">4 rows <i class="mtbl-c">&#9662;</i></span></summary><div class="famtbl mtbl-t"><table><tr><th style="width:44%">Specification</th><th>Polaris Essentials</th></tr><tr><td>Power</td><td class="y">12 VDC, 2 A, 24 W max from the barrel jack; no PoE on Essentials &mdash; the Pod takes PoE+, the Mini does not [Essentials data sheet DS-MCS.ESS-02192025]. <b>The power supply is in the box</b> &mdash; on Pro it is sold separately, because a Pro room is usually powered over Ethernet</td></tr><tr><td>Dimensions &middot; weight</td><td class="y">58 &times; 140 &times; 30 mm [Mersive hardware requirement, rev. Nov 2024] &middot; [weight unverified - hardware team]. Essentials and Pro are <b>different chassis</b>: Pro is 86 &times; 184 &times; 30 mm [Ruled by Damian Blazy 26 Aug 2026]. Both released data sheets print 150 &times; 67 &times; 30 mm and 204 g for both products, which cannot be true of two different enclosures; the sheets are being reissued [verify: mounting options and bracket accessories - product marketing]</td></tr><tr><td>Operating environment</td><td>Indoor use only &middot; 0&deg;C (32&deg;F) to 35&deg;C (95&deg;F) &middot; 30% to 70% RH ambient [Essentials data sheet DS-MCS.ESS-02192025]</td></tr><tr><td>Storage environment</td><td>-20&deg;C (-4&deg;F) to 65&deg;C (149&deg;F) &middot; 10% to 90% RH [Mersive hardware requirement, rev. Nov 2024]</td></tr><tr><td>Regulatory</td><td>Required for launch: FCC (USA) &middot; UL (USA/Canada) &middot; CB Scheme / IECEE (international) &middot; UKCA (UK) &middot; CE (EU) &middot; RoHS (EU) &middot; SCIP (EU) [Mersive hardware requirement, rev. Nov 2024] [verify: IC (Innovation Canada) was published here but appears in neither hardware requirement document - regulatory and legal] [verify: no TAA or NDAA statement appears on either released data sheet - legal and supply chain]</td></tr></table></div></details>
  </Fragment>

  <Fragment slot="upsell">
    <div class="g3band reveal"><b>When a room outgrows Essentials:</b> Polaris Pro adds true wireless BYOM conferencing, HDMI input and dual 4K60 output: same platform, same muscle memory, so stepping up is a pod swap, not a forklift. Cross-network sharing is on both. <a class="btn accent" href="/products/pro">Step up to Pro →</a></div>
  </Fragment>
</ProductShowcase>
